#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e483cc2-b46f-5512-a9a7-0f10ea8a953d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269394} {:answer/id #uuid "4288a7d7-b334-57bd-981d-9101054485a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269869} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf5a7d9-2cd0-5edb-8159-5892ad0bc218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269954} {:answer/id #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270242} {:answer/id #uuid "cf364781-4d57-5ea3-a715-fb67ec1553ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270242} :answer-field/id #uuid "672798eb-6f95-52b3-882f-b5bf772f3083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "396a82c3-ba69-5d62-8d39-94fffd303517" :question/math-academy-id "q-141160" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨ia,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨ia,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument and linearity in the first argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}.$ Therefore, if $a\\ne 0,$ then\n\n$$\n⟨ia,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "810eb785-3105-5975-9d80-b258acff6f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2 + 3\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133339} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6f1ea6-b239-55c6-b125-b1ee37d53b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133340} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea8b206-2f5a-5728-85d0-775f93c6b4d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1 + 5\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133341} {:answer/id #uuid "25210f54-e70c-57c2-b787-ed11cf217cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 - 2\\textrm{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133342} {:answer/id #uuid "639e52ed-2b61-5e63-8d3f-b787a51c279f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133343}] :answer-field/id #uuid "40c24705-9675-595c-b90f-8a554d486556" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cda7ef33-fa7f-5bfa-b9a6-e5261e06d70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382622} {:answer/id #uuid "944aaef0-c92b-5460-b4bf-078c629fb5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382623} {:answer/id #uuid "0a96dcaf-f1c7-527a-b08a-1d68a986f634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382624} {:answer/id #uuid "27ad374f-3361-5475-8073-b762bb17dd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382625} {:answer/id #uuid "14945f42-7413-59f1-969e-cad3bb3baa75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27ad374f-3361-5475-8073-b762bb17dd5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382625} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbcb91a3-9ab8-5494-a94e-36070be9094c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f2e5445-afeb-5118-844a-129faadbb234" :question/math-academy-id "q-96821" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=1+\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,b-(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,b⟩-⟨a,(1+3\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(\\overset{―}{1+3\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,b⟩-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}-(1-3\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{1+\\text{i}}-(1-3\\text{i})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}-2+6\\text{i} \\\\  & =-1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e841129-5370-59a4-a247-981a63a55e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357872} {:answer/id #uuid "0f6fe134-1090-5f97-9ea8-128ddfc3b774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357876} {:answer/id #uuid "c020effb-ac1b-5c1a-b136-a3f990641cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359435} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7d8484-175b-5e8d-bc3b-f162579d8e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359541} {:answer/id #uuid "e819a706-cc55-53d4-ad1b-913fba1a9b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb7d8484-175b-5e8d-bc3b-f162579d8e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359541} :answer-field/id #uuid "a14982e8-8062-5f22-9abd-32b12535e617" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34140471-4f5c-5a2d-9e46-e76840a3d73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97193" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩$ if  \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=-1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩ & =⟨a,(4-\\text{i})b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c\\text{ }⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1-\\text{i}})+5 \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (-1+\\text{i})+5 \\\\  & =-5+3\\text{i}+5 \\\\  & =3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b1189c0-3251-5c7c-817b-7a39c0cfb5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358541} {:answer/id #uuid "91265db6-dce7-5eec-9519-3d3a281089d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359026} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b75f4a-48bb-5311-bdcf-94fee8436a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359360} {:answer/id #uuid "da05ae5f-5078-577d-94a3-569381365c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359660} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0dd3c8-34b2-590d-8552-ba6dcd34cd6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb0dd3c8-34b2-590d-8552-ba6dcd34cd6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359973} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a26644-5b9a-583b-8d85-eae43b25ea9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fac0a4e-d3e8-507c-b2ff-dcb288cf0e43" :question/math-academy-id "q-97258" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fdcdad3-e1b1-5a7a-b43d-f503abcdcd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358030} {:answer/id #uuid "47bb2880-6105-5884-a910-6ff3fb40c799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358369} {:answer/id #uuid "b33621c1-471e-5f87-b4dd-e6a4f7c2271e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359313} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b5ffce-32b5-5567-8de2-f6e5178d0127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359611} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2853e0-f5e9-5edf-9276-d50fe0a40a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fdcdad3-e1b1-5a7a-b43d-f503abcdcd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358030} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ef80d06-a51d-581a-a204-f6dcc0201009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "354bca31-ea8a-5e1d-a896-d3e34e677d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-97296" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
